course: dictionary
lesson: 14961
title: A formal word: relinquishment
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Relinquishment is a long, formal word. You will meet it in history, law and citizenship, and it means the act of giving up something. You can read it in pieces: relinquish means let go or give up, and the ending -ment shows it is the act of doing that.
explain: «The king's relinquishment of the throne surprised the whole country.» The words around it give the clues. A throne is a king's seat of power, and the country was surprised, so the king must have given it up. Try the swap: «The king's giving up of the throne surprised the whole country.» It still makes sense.
explain: Relinquishment often means giving up something unwillingly, such as power, control or a right. «The rebels made a relinquishment of the castle only after the siege ended.» They did not want to give it up, but they had to. When you meet a stiff, formal word, look for the everyday words it could be swapped for: giving up, handing over.
fact: Relinquishment is a noun, and it is usually uncountable: you say «the relinquishment of power», not «a relinquishment».
fact: Relinquishment means the act of giving up something, such as power, a right, a claim or control.
fact: In law, relinquishment can mean a parent formally giving up their rights to a child, or someone giving up their citizenship.
fact: Everyday words for relinquishment are giving up and handing over.
fact: Common phrases are «relinquishment of power» and «relinquishment of control».
exercise: «The king's relinquishment of the throne surprised everyone.» What does relinquishment mean here? | Building a new castle | Winning a great battle | *Giving up the throne | Holding a royal feast
exercise: Relinquishment is a formal word. What is a simpler everyday phrase for it? | *Handing over | Growing bigger | Falling asleep | Singing loudly
exercise: Which everyday word can you swap in for relinquishment in «the relinquishment of control»? | Counting | *Giving up | Shouting | Sleeping
test: «The company's relinquishment of control to its workers was unexpected.» What does relinquishment suggest? | Forgetting the workers | Buying a new company | *Giving up control | Taking more control
test: In law, a parent's relinquishment of rights to a child means the parent | *Formally gives up their rights | Keeps all their rights | Sings to the child | Gets a new child
exam: «The rebels' relinquishment of the castle ended the siege.» What did the rebels do? | Built a new castle | *Gave up the castle | Visited the castle | Won the castle
